 "equations": [
  {
   "name": "Power dissipation",
   "expr": "PD = (VIN - VOUT) × IOUT + VIN × IGND",
   "where": {
    "PD": "power dissipated in the regulator",
    "VIN": "input voltage",
    "VOUT": "output voltage",
    "IOUT": "load current",
    "IGND": "ground pin current"
   },
   "note": "With ground current under 100 µA across the load range its term is below 1 % and is dropped. Worked case: VIN = 3.6 V, VOUT = 2.8 V, IOUT = 300 mA gives PD = 0.240 W.",
   "page": 9
  },
  {
   "name": "Maximum power dissipation",
   "expr": "PD(max) = (TJ(max) - TA) / θJA",
   "where": {
    "TJ(max)": "maximum junction temperature, 125 °C",
    "TA": "ambient temperature",
    "θJA": "thermal resistance from junction to ambient: 250 °C/W for the thin DFN on a minimum footprint, 253 °C/W for SOT23"
   },
   "note": "Setting PD(max) equal to the dissipation and solving for TA gives the highest ambient. Worked case: 0.240 W in the thin DFN on a minimum footprint gives TA = 65 °C.",
   "page": 9
  }
 ],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Inside the part",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "A reference block and the LDO block sit between VIN, EN, VOUT and GND; EN feeds both the reference and the LDO."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Enable and shutdown",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "EN is active high. Driving it low turns the regulator off, and supply current drops to 0.05 µA typical (1 µA max). Driving it high turns the output on; the output is up within 50 µs typical (125 µs max) with 1 µF on the output and 150 mA load. EN reads low at 0.2 V or less and high at 1.2 V or more. On the MIC5501 and MIC5502 EN is a CMOS input and an open pin can leave the output in an undefined state."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Enable pull-down (MIC5503, MIC5504)",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "An internal pull-down of 4 MΩ typical on EN keeps the output off when the control line floats or is tri-stated, for example while a processor boots. It raises the EN input current to 1.4 µA typical (2 µA max) at VEN = 5.5 V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Output auto-discharge (MIC5502, MIC5504)",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "While disabled, an internal N-channel switch of 25 Ω typical connects VOUT to ground and drains the output capacitor."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Input capacitor",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "A 1 µF capacitor from VIN to GND is needed for stability. Low-ESR ceramic parts in X5R or X7R suit it; Y5V is not recommended because it loses most of its capacitance over temperature. Small NPO capacitors added alongside help filter high-frequency noise in RF circuits."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Output capacitor",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "VOUT needs 1 µF or more for stability, ideally a low-ESR ceramic chip capacitor; high-ESR capacitors can make the loop oscillate at high frequency. Performance is tuned for 1 µF and does not improve much with more. X7R and X5R are recommended: X7R varies by 15 % over temperature, while Z5U and Y5V can move by up to 50 % and 60 %, so a Y5V part has to be much larger to keep the same minimum capacitance."
  },
  {
   "topic": "No-load stability",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "With zero load current the output still holds its regulated value and the loop remains stable, which suits CMOS RAM keep-alive supplies."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Protection",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "Linear current limiting (630 mA typical into a shorted output, 400 mA min, 900 mA max) and thermal shutdown protect the part from fault conditions."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Thermal design",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "The part is rated for 300 mA continuous. Dissipation is the input-output drop times the load current; the ground current adds less than 1 %. With θJA of 250 °C/W on a minimum footprint, a 1 mm × 1 mm DFN dropping 0.8 V at 300 mA (0.24 W) may run in ambients up to 65 °C."
  }
 ],
